        "presentation_metadata": {
          "title": "Source edits update conditional recall",
          "question": "How do local source changes update P(B=0 | A=0) exactly? Entry: Reader knows a document can state a weighted group of observations and can read a conditional fraction. Introduce binary fields A,B and fixed order A then B. Exit: Reader can trace changed/added/removed source contributions to numerator/denominator and exact recalled fraction; knows source provenance, conditional support and incremental work are separate from full-rebuild verification.",
          "caption": "Actual source statements and revised conditional recall in the controlled weighted-record corpus. Tuple order(A,B), selected population A=0 and recorded event B=0. Both n00=4 and n01=1 initially contribute. Replacing4by3 changes root mass4/9 and suffix3/4; adding a distinct batch gives1/2 and3/5, and removing OLD batch01 retracts only its contribution. The affected root/A0/leaf changes while A1suffix is reused. Exact witness spans and complete verification/cost details are in the paper's supporting appendix. This is explanatory repair from unchanged accepted data, not a new language extraction or benchmark result.",
          "source_data": null,
          "source": "evidence/DYNAMIC_MEMORY_REWRITE_2026-11-11/source-to-message-final.tikz"
